nvk: command memory the GPU reads is GPU-written video memory; CPU-written command memory is system memory.

Follows nvk-cpu-written-memory-placement.patch: after it, the only driver-owned video memory the CPU still
wrote through BAR1 was command upload memory (root descriptor tables, QMDs, push descriptor sets, and the
CPU uploads of vkCmdUpdateBuffer/meta), the zero page and, before Maxwell B, the QMD heap.

The stock driver (libnvidia-eglcore 615.71.09) never writes video memory with the CPU: its pushbuffer is
system memory (write-combined, GPU-uncached) and the per-dispatch constants and descriptor data are written
into a video-memory arena by the GPU, with inline-to-memory from the pushbuffer (LINE_LENGTH_IN, LINE_COUNT,
OFFSET_OUT, LAUNCH_DMA = PITCH | FLUSH_DISABLE | SYSMEMBAR_DISABLE, LOAD_INLINE_DATA), immediately followed
by the QMD and the launch. Do the same:

* VRAM command memory is never CPU-mapped. Root descriptor tables, QMDs and push descriptor sets are
  allocated there and written with I2M on the subchannel that consumes them. Only the 64-byte chunks of the
  root table that were written since the last state reset are copied (the others are zero and not
  referenced by valid commands). When the data is patched by the GPU afterwards (indirect dispatch) or read
  by a shader (generated commands), the I2M flushes (COMPLETION_TYPE_FLUSH_ONLY, as nvk_CmdUpdateBuffer).
  I2M is subject to conditional rendering, so these writes are bracketed with
  SET_RENDER_ENABLE_OVERRIDE = ALWAYS_RENDER, restoring meta's override.
* The root table is written before the dispatch's dependency links (nvk-cs-join-qmd.patch), the QMD after
  them and before the launch, where stock loads its dispatch QMD inline. The indirect dispatch that loads
  its QMD inline (nvk-compute-queue-no-mme.patch) gets the QMD back instead of an I2M copy.
* CPU uploads (nvk_cmd_buffer_upload_alloc: meta buffers, large vkCmdUpdateBuffer data, SKED counters) and
  the conditional-rendering scratch (both queue families) are system memory.
* The zero page is video memory without a CPU mapping, zeroed through the upload queue; the pre-Maxwell-B
  QMD heap is not CPU-mapped.

Cost: command memory in system memory instead costs 3.4x GPU time on a dispatch-heavy command buffer
(every QMD and root-table fetch crosses PCIe uncached); I2M into video memory does not.

